00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:29.220
So for those of you who
aren't aware of the issues

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:31.380
with industrial data
sets, we're essentially

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:34.770
working on sensor information
coming off of big machines.

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:38.440
And sensors are really
inconsistent and untrustworthy.

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:40.470
There's sparse labels,
so people are not

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:43.230
sitting there labeling all of
the information that comes off

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:46.060
of it, which is mostly required
for adequate machine learning.

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:48.990
And it's not prepped for
adequate data science.

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:51.240
So if we look at two
separate wind turbines--

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:53.070
I go through the
wind turbine example

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:54.630
because it's a lot
easier for people

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:57.410
to understand relative
to oil and gas examples.

00:09:57.410 --> 00:09:59.160
But essentially, if
you have wind turbines

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:00.900
in two separate
locations, those are

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:03.810
going to require two separate
machine learning models.

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:06.480
A wind turbine in Location A
might have a different failure

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:11.490
profile or predictive profile
than one in Location B.

00:10:11.490 --> 00:10:14.670
An operator that runs a wind
farm might operate their farm

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:17.980
differently than another one.

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:21.060
The sensor packages that
are on a wind turbine

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:22.170
will be different.

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:23.940
And the actual turbine
that's actually

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:26.910
running in the wind
farm, those will

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:29.102
be different between
two separate locations.

00:10:29.102 --> 00:10:30.060
So what does that mean?

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:32.393
That actually means that you
cannot build one model that

00:10:32.393 --> 00:10:34.470
will run on both of these.

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:36.420
There needs to be a
scalable solution--

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:38.700
an automated solution-- that
can build specific models

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:41.480
for each individual asset.

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:43.750
So when we looked at this
problem when we started out,

00:10:43.750 --> 00:10:45.640
and we started receiving
a lot of information

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:48.970
from industrial companies
looking for data science work,

00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:51.460
we ended up having too
many customers, too

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:53.980
many customer requests for
the existing data that we had.

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:55.729
So these were some of
the internal factors

00:10:55.729 --> 00:11:00.220
that we were looking at, to need
to build an automated solution.

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:02.200
In addition, to
actually build a model

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:04.480
for each individual
turbine, that's

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:07.360
a time-intensive process.

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:09.970
The current solutions that we're
offering were not scalable,

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:12.850
and there was clearly a lack
of an automated solution

00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:15.700
on the market that actually
could do predictions

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:18.040
for each individual asset.

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:20.260
Some of the external
factors that point us

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:22.624
to automation-- and I went
through a lot of these,

00:11:22.624 --> 00:11:24.790
but there's a lot of variation
from different assets

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:28.030
depending on location,
what's on the assets and all

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:29.025
that kind of stuff.

00:11:29.025 --> 00:11:30.400
In addition, you
needed something

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:35.570
to solve problems with sparse
data and noisy data as well.

00:11:35.570 --> 00:11:38.500
So when we saw all of these
problems, we went through

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:41.290
and we said, OK, let's look
at the workflow of a data

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:44.560
scientist, and figure out
what we can automate around.

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:46.810
So for a data scientist,
the first thing that you do

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:49.180
is you go through a
data cleaning step.

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:50.212
You take your data.

00:11:50.212 --> 00:11:52.420
You do any sort of imputation,
or any sort of methods

00:11:52.420 --> 00:11:55.540
that sort of make it into
a functional data set.

00:11:55.540 --> 00:11:57.790
Then you go through your
feature engineering practice,

00:11:57.790 --> 00:11:59.230
and you try to figure
out what features

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:00.896
you need to engineer,
what you can drop,

00:12:00.896 --> 00:12:03.250
and all of those sorts
of little nuances

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:06.539
that's sort of the magic of
one specific data scientist.

00:12:06.539 --> 00:12:08.080
And then you need
to build the model.

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:10.000
And if that model
doesn't work, depending

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:11.500
on what tools you
use, you go back

00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:13.120
and you revisit your
cleaning methods.

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:15.709
You go back and you revisit your
feature engineering methods.

00:12:15.709 --> 00:12:17.500
And you go back and
you revisit your model.

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:19.870
And its inherently just
an iterative process

00:12:19.870 --> 00:12:21.700
that can take a very long time.

00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:24.040
Now on two separate
wind turbines,

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:25.900
maybe you could
expedite this by just

00:12:25.900 --> 00:12:28.730
having background knowledge
of what usually works.

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:33.160
But in essence, you will need
a person or a data science team

00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:36.200
to actually build a model
for each individual asset.

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:39.290
So all of this requires a high
amount of technical expertise,

00:12:39.290 --> 00:12:40.300
a lot of time.

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:42.790
And there was a lack
of productization tools

00:12:42.790 --> 00:12:46.682
out there that could
solve all these problems.

00:12:46.682 --> 00:12:48.640
So we went out, and we
tried to build something

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:51.160
that could automate the
data cleaning process,

00:12:51.160 --> 00:12:53.260
automate the feature
engineering process,

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:56.780
and especially automate
the model building process.

00:12:56.780 --> 00:12:58.780
So I'm going to take a
quick technical segue

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:00.760
to talk about how we
went through this,

00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:04.240
and I hope that everyone
can stick with me on this.

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:07.450
So when we talk about automating
the feature engineering process

00:13:07.450 --> 00:13:09.710
and cleaning step, I'll
get to that in a second.

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:11.860
But our approach towards
model building, this

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:14.340
is an image of a neural network.

00:13:14.340 --> 00:13:17.220
There are three layers inside
of this neural network.

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:20.400
And each circle is essentially
math happening at each circle.

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:23.490
And each line, you can think
of that as a coefficient that

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:25.630
happens at each line.

00:13:25.630 --> 00:13:27.340
Now what a data
scientist did is they

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:29.107
designed what this looks like.

00:13:29.107 --> 00:13:31.690
So where all those circles are,
and where all those lines are,

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:34.810
a data scientist said, this is
the structure of the network

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:35.866
that I want.

00:13:35.866 --> 00:13:37.240
And what machine
learning does is

00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:39.790
it goes and just tries to
figure out what weights

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:43.400
or what coefficients happen
at each one of those lines.

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:45.880
Now if this is not the
optimal network structure,

00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:48.430
there is no way to determine
what actually might be.

00:13:48.430 --> 00:13:52.060
It's just your leverage to
whatever data scientist is

00:13:52.060 --> 00:13:54.190
inherent knowledge or
whatever is in a library that

00:13:54.190 --> 00:13:56.090
already exists.

00:13:56.090 --> 00:13:58.690
So what we did was
how can we automate

00:13:58.690 --> 00:14:00.820
the search for the
right network topology,

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:06.310
or what structure actually works
best for a specific data set.

00:14:06.310 --> 00:14:08.220
So we went through,
and we built a product

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:09.880
that leverages neuroevolution.

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:12.580
So it uses
evolutionary algorithms

00:14:12.580 --> 00:14:15.490
to actually search for the
network architecture that

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:17.080
solves this problem.

00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:19.510
And it not only searches for
the network architecture,

00:14:19.510 --> 00:14:22.014
it chooses different imputation
methods for cleaning.

00:14:22.014 --> 00:14:24.430
It chooses different features
after features are generated

00:14:24.430 --> 00:14:28.420
automatically, and it searches
for the network optimization

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:31.270
architecture that is
perfect for or optimized

00:14:31.270 --> 00:14:34.252
for the actual data set
that it's being trained on.

00:14:34.252 --> 00:14:35.210
So how does it do this?

00:14:35.210 --> 00:14:37.130
So you upload your data set.

00:14:37.130 --> 00:14:39.490
And in the first
generation you build

00:14:39.490 --> 00:14:41.680
70 different neural networks.

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:44.980
And we also combined it with
some tree-based algorithms.

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:47.470
And in that generation,
we look at what

00:14:47.470 --> 00:14:50.800
are the genomes of those
specific models that

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:54.250
worked the best to make
those models accurate.

00:14:54.250 --> 00:14:57.340
Those genomes then get mutated
into the next generation,

00:14:57.340 --> 00:15:02.380
and spawn 70 new neural networks
off of that initial population.

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:04.150
And we go through
that process again.

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:07.510
We take the genomes from
the best-performing models,

00:15:07.510 --> 00:15:10.970
we mutate and breed them
into the next generation.

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:14.620
And we spawn off 70
new models again.

00:15:14.620 --> 00:15:17.480
And that happens for n
number of generations.

00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:19.840
So in this case, now you
can upload your data set,

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:23.470
and it will do all of the
feature engineering, the data

00:15:23.470 --> 00:15:25.960
cleaning process, and
the network search--

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:27.670
the network
architecture search--

00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:30.046
specific to the data set
that you upload it to.

00:15:30.046 --> 00:15:31.420
And this is really
important when

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:35.330
we talk about things like wind
turbine A versus wind turbine

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:37.600
B. You're actually
doing a lot of the steps

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:41.750
specific to the asset that
it's being trained upon.

00:15:41.750 --> 00:15:43.670
So what do these networks
end up looking like?

00:15:43.670 --> 00:15:45.280
So here is three
separate use cases.

00:15:45.280 --> 00:15:47.110
It's essentially
use case agnostic.

00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:49.610
The one on the left
predicts bearing failure.

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:52.720
That's the architecture that was
trained off of 400 generations.

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:55.660
The one in the middle we
trained to essentially mimic

00:15:55.660 --> 00:15:58.930
one of our in-house
pilots using a flight

00:15:58.930 --> 00:16:02.290
simulator to try and determine
exactly how his flight

00:16:02.290 --> 00:16:02.980
path works.

00:16:02.980 --> 00:16:05.438
And the one on the right, that
is kind of blacked out here,

00:16:05.438 --> 00:16:06.700
trades Dow futures.

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:08.241
So when you talk
about deep learning,

00:16:08.241 --> 00:16:10.300
you can see how big
a model can actually

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:12.160
get by just doing this
generative process,

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:17.000
and running it for however
many generations you see fit.

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:18.980
So again, it's
use case agnostic.

00:16:18.980 --> 00:16:21.790
It's not necessarily specific
to industrial applications,

00:16:21.790 --> 00:16:23.470
although that's
where our DNA is.

00:16:23.470 --> 00:16:26.080
We've kind of grown up
in the industrial space,

00:16:26.080 --> 00:16:28.667
working on wind turbines
and oil and gas assets.

00:16:28.667 --> 00:16:30.250
But with that, I'm
going to kind of go

00:16:30.250 --> 00:16:32.620
through what the oil
and gas application is

00:16:32.620 --> 00:16:35.720
that we've used this for.

00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:39.200
So for those of you who are not
familiar with the oil industry,

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:41.660
there is a concept
called artificial lift.

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:44.510
So when you first
produce from an oil well,

00:16:44.510 --> 00:16:46.610
the natural
pressures of the well

00:16:46.610 --> 00:16:48.350
will lift fluids to the surface.

00:16:48.350 --> 00:16:51.200
So if you've ever seen those
images of oil spewing out

00:16:51.200 --> 00:16:52.882
from the top of
a derrick, that's

00:16:52.882 --> 00:16:54.590
because the natural
pressure of the earth

00:16:54.590 --> 00:16:56.480
is pushing that oil up.

00:16:56.480 --> 00:16:57.980
Now after a certain
amount of time

00:16:57.980 --> 00:17:00.200
the pressure starts to
subside, you produced a lot

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:03.920
from a reservoir, you need a way
to be able to lift that oil out

00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:05.250
of the ground.

00:17:05.250 --> 00:17:08.030
So the sucker rod pump on the
left, there is about a million

00:17:08.030 --> 00:17:10.240
of these in operation
around the world.

00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:13.130
What this is essentially
doing is if you've ever

00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:15.170
had a cup of water
with a straw in it,

00:17:15.170 --> 00:17:17.420
you put your thumb over
the straw and lift up,

00:17:17.420 --> 00:17:19.700
and you see the column
of fluid come up with it,

00:17:19.700 --> 00:17:21.980
it's essentially just
doing that on repeat.

00:17:21.980 --> 00:17:25.060
So it's just kind of sucking all
of the oil out of the ground.

00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:27.500
An electric submersible
pump is essentially a pump

00:17:27.500 --> 00:17:29.140
that you've lowered
into the ground.

00:17:29.140 --> 00:17:33.230
And it turns and pumps
oil to the surface.

00:17:33.230 --> 00:17:37.280
So at a certain point
in the production

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:39.500
of a reservoir, after the
pressure has subsided,

00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:41.990
you can no longer
produce to the surface,

00:17:41.990 --> 00:17:46.030
you start implementing all of
these artificial lift systems.

00:17:46.030 --> 00:17:49.060
And with these there are
many different manufacturers.

00:17:49.060 --> 00:17:52.150
They've been in place
since the early 1940s.

00:17:52.150 --> 00:17:54.520
There is a variety
in every field--

00:17:54.520 --> 00:17:57.700
the same aspects of what I went
through with the wind turbines.

00:17:57.700 --> 00:17:59.890
Different fields have
different profiles.

00:17:59.890 --> 00:18:03.280
Different sensors are put
on each piece of machine.

00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:05.680
And different operators have
different tactics in order

00:18:05.680 --> 00:18:07.480
to make sure that
they're optimizing

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:10.880
these specific assets.

00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:13.640
So with this, we
worked with a company--

00:18:13.640 --> 00:18:15.760
one of our partners, Apergy.

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:20.150
Apergy is one of
the biggest software

00:18:20.150 --> 00:18:22.796
providers that actually
monitors a lot of these assets.

00:18:22.796 --> 00:18:24.920
So you can think about a
monitoring software that's

00:18:24.920 --> 00:18:27.710
streaming information directly
from these artificial lift

00:18:27.710 --> 00:18:31.589
systems into their platform.

00:18:31.589 --> 00:18:34.130
And all of their data kind of
sits inside of their monitoring

00:18:34.130 --> 00:18:34.970
software.

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:36.770
And production analysts
and specialists

00:18:36.770 --> 00:18:39.380
look through this
information, and try and make

00:18:39.380 --> 00:18:41.630
determinations on
what's going to fail,

00:18:41.630 --> 00:18:44.820
what's going to optimize,
those sorts of things.

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:48.270
Apergy also sits across
wide-ranging applications

00:18:48.270 --> 00:18:52.232
both in drilling for oil,
as well as producing it.

00:18:52.232 --> 00:18:53.940
So even though we're
talking specifically

00:18:53.940 --> 00:18:56.010
about these artificial
lift systems here,

00:18:56.010 --> 00:18:57.593
we're eventually
going to start moving

00:18:57.593 --> 00:19:01.000
into more and more of these
really complex applications.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:03.645
You can go all the way up to
drilling applications as well.

00:19:03.645 --> 00:19:05.520
But for this, as you
see circled on the left,

00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:08.385
we're focusing on the
artificial lift systems.

00:19:11.330 --> 00:19:13.400
So the way that this
works in practice

00:19:13.400 --> 00:19:16.820
is that an ESP in the field
will stream data directly

00:19:16.820 --> 00:19:20.457
into an Apergy
software, where someone

00:19:20.457 --> 00:19:22.040
can make interpretations
off of what's

00:19:22.040 --> 00:19:25.610
actually happening inside of
the well and make decisions.

00:19:25.610 --> 00:19:28.730
And then we'll take that
data either through an API

00:19:28.730 --> 00:19:32.150
or through just a downloaded
CSV from their software,

00:19:32.150 --> 00:19:34.340
and put it into
Darwin, where that

00:19:34.340 --> 00:19:35.960
will build a predictive model.

00:19:35.960 --> 00:19:37.850
And Darwin sits in Google Cloud.

00:19:37.850 --> 00:19:39.350
That model that can
then be put back

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:42.440
into Apergy's software,
and any user, any producer

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:44.480
can actually just make
determinations off

00:19:44.480 --> 00:19:46.400
of the prediction model.

00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:51.987
Now for us, Darwin, we have
it as a standalone software.

00:19:51.987 --> 00:19:53.570
And I'm going to go
through that demo.

00:19:53.570 --> 00:19:55.610
You can upload CSVs
just to the software

00:19:55.610 --> 00:19:56.760
that sits on the cloud.

00:19:56.760 --> 00:19:59.810
Or you can leverage it in
sort of an API call connection

00:19:59.810 --> 00:20:04.770
to any sort of monitoring
software there.

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:08.760
Now here I talk about this
specific monitoring software.

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:12.340
But you can imagine it being
any sort of monitoring software

00:20:12.340 --> 00:20:17.190
or business intelligence tool
that you want to put it into.

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:19.450
I had an executive come
to me and say, well,

00:20:19.450 --> 00:20:22.320
in the context here, what do
you guys mean about BI tool?

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:24.210
And I said, you don't even know.

00:20:24.210 --> 00:20:27.180
We're thinking so far past AI.

00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:28.606
No, that didn't hit that well?

00:20:28.606 --> 00:20:32.310
[CHUCKLING]

00:20:32.310 --> 00:20:33.560
BEN WILSON: That's a bad joke.

00:20:33.560 --> 00:20:34.970
That's just a bad joke.

00:20:34.970 --> 00:20:35.910
BRANT SWIDLER: No one
was prepped for it.

00:20:35.910 --> 00:20:37.071
BEN WILSON: We weren't
sure if it was a good joke,

00:20:37.071 --> 00:20:37.900
but now that I see it--

00:20:37.900 --> 00:20:40.066
BRANT SWIDLER: I thought
that that hit a lot harder.

00:20:40.066 --> 00:20:42.031
Already thinking past AI.

00:20:42.031 --> 00:20:43.530
So I'll go through
the demonstration

00:20:43.530 --> 00:20:47.050
of how this actually works.

00:20:47.050 --> 00:20:48.930
So I'm going to
upload this data set.

00:20:48.930 --> 00:20:50.970
What this data set is is
all of the information

00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:53.219
that's coming off of this
electric submersible pump.

00:20:53.219 --> 00:20:54.760
This is an electric
submersible pump.

00:20:54.760 --> 00:20:56.100
So it's the one that you
lower into the ground,

00:20:56.100 --> 00:20:57.501
and it pumps oil to surface.

00:20:57.501 --> 00:20:59.250
What we're trying to
do is do a prediction

00:20:59.250 --> 00:21:02.710
on how much oil it will produce
within the next seven days.

00:21:02.710 --> 00:21:05.760
So off of the sensor,
we're getting some metadata

00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:09.110
on where this well is actually
located, production figures--

00:21:09.110 --> 00:21:11.330
so oil, water, gas.

00:21:11.330 --> 00:21:12.960
An oil well doesn't
just produce oil.

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:16.170
It produces a mixture of oil,
water, gas, and other sorts

00:21:16.170 --> 00:21:18.039
of dead dinosaur stuff.

00:21:18.039 --> 00:21:19.830
And then you also have
the tubing pressure,

00:21:19.830 --> 00:21:22.500
casing pressure, and all of the
sensors coming off of the pump.

00:21:22.500 --> 00:21:24.083
And again, what we're
trying to get to

00:21:24.083 --> 00:21:25.860
is just a very simple
prediction of what

00:21:25.860 --> 00:21:27.390
the next seven-day
forecast will be.

00:21:36.570 --> 00:21:40.320
So within our software-- and
this is the software version--

00:21:40.320 --> 00:21:41.820
you can also set
it up where it just

00:21:41.820 --> 00:21:44.160
builds models automatically
through a connection.

00:21:44.160 --> 00:21:45.576
But what we're
going to go through

00:21:45.576 --> 00:21:47.620
is here I've already
built all of these models.

00:21:47.620 --> 00:21:50.280
They're basically cards that
sit on side the software.

00:21:57.276 --> 00:22:01.320
Let's see if it starts playing.

00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:02.846
I go Create New Model.

00:22:02.846 --> 00:22:05.220
And I can upload or name the
model that I want to create.

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:07.380
In this case it's Seven
Day Production Forecast.

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:11.300
I can give it a description.

00:22:16.910 --> 00:22:20.110
And then I'm given all
of these workflows-- so

00:22:20.110 --> 00:22:25.790
Infer Values, Forecast
Values, Infer Categories, Find

00:22:25.790 --> 00:22:28.452
Patterns, or Predict Events.

00:22:28.452 --> 00:22:30.035
So in this case,
we're trying to build

00:22:30.035 --> 00:22:31.620
a library of these
sorts of cards

00:22:31.620 --> 00:22:33.855
that, depending on the
workflow people want,

00:22:33.855 --> 00:22:37.170
this can sort of organize
the data set in that fashion.

00:22:37.170 --> 00:22:39.770
Infer Values is a
simple regression.

00:22:39.770 --> 00:22:43.580
Forecast Values is just
shifting that sort target column

00:22:43.580 --> 00:22:46.510
to be able to forecast, predict
something in the future.

00:22:46.510 --> 00:22:50.170
Infer Categories, this
is a classification.

00:22:50.170 --> 00:22:52.790
Find Patterns is our
clustering approach.

00:22:52.790 --> 00:22:56.360
And Predict Events
is our strategy

00:22:56.360 --> 00:23:00.000
towards predicting
a point in time.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:01.640
In this case, we're
going to go ahead,

00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:05.450
and we're going to infer values.

00:23:05.450 --> 00:23:06.470
So I go to this card.

00:23:10.730 --> 00:23:13.760
And I'll click Infer Values.

00:23:13.760 --> 00:23:16.720
If you have any questions on the
software, you can go through.

00:23:16.720 --> 00:23:18.190
And here I'm pointed to--

00:23:18.190 --> 00:23:20.290
I want to upload it, drag
and drop my data set.

00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:25.020
Give a little
description of what

00:23:25.020 --> 00:23:26.270
the data set should look like.

00:23:26.270 --> 00:23:27.686
So we try and
formulate everything

00:23:27.686 --> 00:23:29.560
in these rectangular
formats where,

00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:33.550
if you think about this
as an Excel file, or CSV,

00:23:33.550 --> 00:23:36.430
all of your inputs are
in rows, or in columns,

00:23:36.430 --> 00:23:38.230
and every timestamp is in rows.

00:23:38.230 --> 00:23:39.670
And one of your
predicted outputs

00:23:39.670 --> 00:23:42.280
is in the target column.

00:23:50.757 --> 00:23:51.965
So you go find your data set.

00:24:06.310 --> 00:24:07.880
You can visualize
your data set here.

00:24:07.880 --> 00:24:10.640
And then you can go ahead and
select the targeted column.

00:24:10.640 --> 00:24:12.806
So in this case, you're
going to go ahead and select

00:24:12.806 --> 00:24:14.740
the column that is Oil
Production Prediction.

00:24:14.740 --> 00:24:15.876
Click Next.

00:24:15.876 --> 00:24:17.500
And then you're driven
to a point where

00:24:17.500 --> 00:24:22.300
you can choose how long you
want to train your model for,

00:24:22.300 --> 00:24:25.180
or how many generations
you actually

00:24:25.180 --> 00:24:28.000
want to go through and
actually build this network

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:28.870
architecture.

00:24:28.870 --> 00:24:31.390
Obviously, the
longer you train for,

00:24:31.390 --> 00:24:32.770
the more it should converge.

00:24:32.770 --> 00:24:34.780
We're giving out metrics
as well to make sure

00:24:34.780 --> 00:24:37.690
that you can see how well
it's converging over time.

00:24:37.690 --> 00:24:41.701
But this, as of right
now, you can just

00:24:41.701 --> 00:24:43.450
determine the amount
of time that you want

00:24:43.450 --> 00:24:45.549
to wait for this to train for.

00:24:45.549 --> 00:24:46.840
There's also advanced features.

00:24:46.840 --> 00:24:48.490
You can change the
accuracy targets.

00:24:48.490 --> 00:24:51.375
For this, we use normalized
root mean squared error.

00:24:51.375 --> 00:24:53.500
But now we're building our
model behind the scenes.

00:24:53.500 --> 00:24:58.660
It's going through this process
of building models, determining

00:24:58.660 --> 00:25:02.020
the right genomes, mutating
those into the next generation,

00:25:02.020 --> 00:25:04.000
and continually going
through that process

00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:05.680
until we derive the
network architecture

00:25:05.680 --> 00:25:08.530
specific to this data set.

00:25:08.530 --> 00:25:10.730
We give out the statistics
on this specific model.

00:25:10.730 --> 00:25:14.140
So you can see how accurate the
models got over a generation

00:25:14.140 --> 00:25:15.490
or over time.

00:25:15.490 --> 00:25:17.260
You can look at all
of the statistics

00:25:17.260 --> 00:25:18.843
around the data set
that you uploaded.

00:25:22.170 --> 00:25:24.420
In addition, you can
eventually get to the point

00:25:24.420 --> 00:25:26.700
where you're looking
at feature importances.

00:25:26.700 --> 00:25:30.870
Here's the graph of
the actual output.

00:25:30.870 --> 00:25:32.850
So you can see your
predicted versus actual,

00:25:32.850 --> 00:25:34.977
and see how that
trends over time.

00:25:34.977 --> 00:25:37.060
And then you can look at
your feature importances.

00:25:37.060 --> 00:25:40.620
Or here you can see
that in an Excel format,

00:25:40.620 --> 00:25:43.369
or CSV format, the
predicted versus actual.

00:25:43.369 --> 00:25:44.910
In this case now
you get to the point

00:25:44.910 --> 00:25:46.909
where you can look at
what features are actually

00:25:46.909 --> 00:25:49.000
driving these predictions.

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:52.740
So this is the model-based
prediction feature importances

00:25:52.740 --> 00:25:56.860
for this specific ESP for the
model for this specific ESP.

00:26:01.350 --> 00:26:02.830
And once you have
your model built,

00:26:02.830 --> 00:26:04.420
you have a number of options.

00:26:04.420 --> 00:26:09.360
Either you can run, meaning
test your model on unseen data.

00:26:09.360 --> 00:26:11.340
You can publish it,
or improve, meaning

00:26:11.340 --> 00:26:13.680
upload your data,
upload new data

00:26:13.680 --> 00:26:15.270
to train the model further.

00:26:15.270 --> 00:26:17.160
You can publish it.

00:26:17.160 --> 00:26:20.100
That means making an API call
to the model that you have.

00:26:20.100 --> 00:26:22.140
Or you can download it
in the pickle file format

00:26:22.140 --> 00:26:24.390
that we have, so you can put
it into a runtime engine.

00:26:26.980 --> 00:26:29.250
And now we're going
to upload a test set.

00:26:29.250 --> 00:26:31.680
So uploading the test set,
you can upload your code

00:26:31.680 --> 00:26:33.480
in the same format
as drag and drop.

00:26:44.950 --> 00:26:46.760
And you have your predictions.

00:26:46.760 --> 00:26:49.520
So here is now the predictions
on the next test set.

00:26:49.520 --> 00:26:52.044
We're doing seven days
production forecasting.

00:26:52.044 --> 00:26:53.460
And now you have
your model built.

00:26:53.460 --> 00:26:55.490
You can see the
testing accuracy.

00:26:55.490 --> 00:26:57.160
And in addition, we
actually give out

00:26:57.160 --> 00:27:00.370
another feature that gives
you a per prediction feature

00:27:00.370 --> 00:27:01.240
importance.

00:27:01.240 --> 00:27:02.830
So when I click
on a value, I can

00:27:02.830 --> 00:27:09.924
see what's driving this
prediction down, less accurate;

00:27:09.924 --> 00:27:11.340
and what's driving
this prediction

00:27:11.340 --> 00:27:12.730
to become more accurate.

00:27:12.730 --> 00:27:14.730
So on a per
prediction basis, now

00:27:14.730 --> 00:27:17.350
you can see what's actually
driving your predictions.

00:27:17.350 --> 00:27:18.979
So here through
this entire tool we

00:27:18.979 --> 00:27:20.520
went through the
process of uploading

00:27:20.520 --> 00:27:22.980
a data set from an
ESP, building a model

00:27:22.980 --> 00:27:25.740
to predict the forecasted
values of the next seven days

00:27:25.740 --> 00:27:29.220
production, and then being able
to look at the model output

00:27:29.220 --> 00:27:31.531
and test it on new
data sets, and see

00:27:31.531 --> 00:27:34.030
each individual prediction, and
what drives that up or down.

00:27:38.900 --> 00:27:40.660
So even more than
that, the way that I

00:27:40.660 --> 00:27:42.760
like to think about this from
the oil and gas perspective

00:27:42.760 --> 00:27:45.100
is what decisions can I
actually make off of this model

00:27:45.100 --> 00:27:47.830
that I've now built. So in this
case, what I've gone and done

00:27:47.830 --> 00:27:51.070
is I have the test set up there.

00:27:51.070 --> 00:27:53.350
And I changed some of
the control parameters

00:27:53.350 --> 00:27:54.860
that I have as an operator.

00:27:54.860 --> 00:27:57.010
So in this case, I can
make the pump pump faster.

00:27:57.010 --> 00:27:58.420
I can make the pump pump slower.

00:27:58.420 --> 00:28:01.370
And I can see how that
impacts my production outcome.

00:28:01.370 --> 00:28:05.230
So the gray line here is the
actual changed production,

00:28:05.230 --> 00:28:07.810
where the orange line is what
was originally predicted.

00:28:07.810 --> 00:28:11.320
So when I make control
parameter changes to my pumps,

00:28:11.320 --> 00:28:14.050
I can see how this impacts
my production figures.

00:28:14.050 --> 00:28:19.956
And now prediction becomes
part of my optimization plan.

00:28:19.956 --> 00:28:21.580
So in this case, the
average production

00:28:21.580 --> 00:28:23.246
increased by changing
the pump parameter

00:28:23.246 --> 00:28:25.502
is increased by 700
barrels per week.

00:28:25.502 --> 00:28:27.460
And now you can go ahead
and just make changes,

00:28:27.460 --> 00:28:32.200
and see how that might impact
the optimization of your ESP.

00:28:32.200 --> 00:28:34.210
Now for those of you who
are oil and gas people,

00:28:34.210 --> 00:28:36.820
you might look at this and
say, well, it's really actually

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:42.970
kind of rare to have a
production figure per well.

00:28:42.970 --> 00:28:47.150
So in actuality,
what happens is you

00:28:47.150 --> 00:28:49.040
have upwards of
60 wells producing

00:28:49.040 --> 00:28:51.080
into one massive tank.

00:28:51.080 --> 00:28:53.690
And inside of that one tank is
where you get your production

00:28:53.690 --> 00:28:54.560
figures.

00:28:54.560 --> 00:28:56.150
So in this case,
you actually have

00:28:56.150 --> 00:28:58.940
all of these wells that
are using different types

00:28:58.940 --> 00:29:00.720
of artificial lift systems.

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:03.230
Different types are
operated possibly

00:29:03.230 --> 00:29:05.210
by different people
in different fields.

00:29:05.210 --> 00:29:10.490
And they're all going
into one battery that's

00:29:10.490 --> 00:29:12.000
the only targeted outcome.

00:29:12.000 --> 00:29:13.530
So for the first
one I went through,

00:29:13.530 --> 00:29:15.590
I had this seven-day
production forecast.

00:29:15.590 --> 00:29:17.330
That seven-day
production forecast

00:29:17.330 --> 00:29:18.620
is specific to that well.

00:29:18.620 --> 00:29:20.420
In actuality you'll
have 60 wells

00:29:20.420 --> 00:29:22.370
that are producing
into that one forecast.

00:29:22.370 --> 00:29:23.930
So now your
optimization is how do I

00:29:23.930 --> 00:29:26.450
change all of these
pump parameters

00:29:26.450 --> 00:29:29.250
in order to make sure that I'm
driving the most production.

00:29:29.250 --> 00:29:31.500
BEN WILSON: Yeah, I mean
for me, for those of you who

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:33.980
have actually operated
batteries, and operated pumps,

00:29:33.980 --> 00:29:35.750
this is the realistic situation.

00:29:35.750 --> 00:29:38.180
I've done a whole
bunch of AI trying

00:29:38.180 --> 00:29:41.300
to do prediction,
and also try to do

00:29:41.300 --> 00:29:43.940
preventative maintenance to try
to be able to predict failures.

00:29:43.940 --> 00:29:46.231
And the biggest challenge is
being able to go out there

00:29:46.231 --> 00:29:47.540
and say, OK, I have a battery.

00:29:47.540 --> 00:29:48.970
It's got 60 wells.

00:29:48.970 --> 00:29:51.154
Odds are all 60 wells
were not drilled by me.

00:29:51.154 --> 00:29:53.570
They were drilled by probably
three or four other parties.

00:29:53.570 --> 00:29:55.520
They have three or four
different types of equipment.

00:29:55.520 --> 00:29:57.150
They have different
types of sensors.

00:29:57.150 --> 00:30:00.480
So being able to have this
iterative approach to going

00:30:00.480 --> 00:30:02.750
and looking at the
data that's coming off,

00:30:02.750 --> 00:30:04.820
and being able to do
predictions by well

00:30:04.820 --> 00:30:06.050
makes a huge difference.

00:30:06.050 --> 00:30:08.341
You can think about it from
an operational perspective,

00:30:08.341 --> 00:30:10.180
and how full is that
tank going to be.

00:30:10.180 --> 00:30:12.680
You can also look at it just
from a maintenance perspective,

00:30:12.680 --> 00:30:13.350
too.

00:30:13.350 --> 00:30:15.580
So when I look at this as
an operator, I look at it

00:30:15.580 --> 00:30:17.540
and say, this is really,
really important.

00:30:17.540 --> 00:30:19.400
One other thing I'd
say, too, is in case

00:30:19.400 --> 00:30:22.550
you don't recognize the name
Apergy, that used to be Dover.

00:30:22.550 --> 00:30:24.440
And most people will
recognize Dover as one

00:30:24.440 --> 00:30:27.532
of the largest artificial lift
manufacturers in the world.

00:30:27.532 --> 00:30:29.240
And when you think
about artificial lift,

00:30:29.240 --> 00:30:32.925
you think about those pushing
them down into the well.

00:30:32.925 --> 00:30:35.300
And usually you put three or
four or five of these things

00:30:35.300 --> 00:30:35.960
together.

00:30:35.960 --> 00:30:38.251
And being able to get these
things to operate correctly

00:30:38.251 --> 00:30:40.820
across a set of
batteries like this--

00:30:40.820 --> 00:30:41.860
really complex.

00:30:41.860 --> 00:30:43.970
So this really does
simplify that process.

